================================================================================================
D1 -- LAW L1 (no-EFE, the task's law): THE a0-C DEGENERACY IS EXACT
================================================================================================
  gamma(g) = (1 + C a0/g)^(1/4).  a0 and C appear ONLY in the product C*a0,
  so d gamma/d lnC == d gamma/d ln a0 POINTWISE, at every separation.
  [canonical] max_b |dgamma/dlnC - dgamma/dln a0| = 2.22e-12 (machine zero)   weighted cos = 1.000000000000000
             Fisher eigenvalues = -1.422e-14, 5.285e+02  -> cond = 5.285e+302  det = 2.391e-13
             null eigenvector (lnC, ln a0) = (+0.7071, -0.7071)  == the C*a0 = const ray
  [ALT      ] max_b |dgamma/dlnC - dgamma/dln a0| = 4.44e-12 (machine zero)   weighted cos = 1.000000000000000
             Fisher eigenvalues = -2.309e-14, 6.489e+02  -> cond = 6.489e+302  det = -1.498e-11
             null eigenvector (lnC, ln a0) = (+0.7071, -0.7071)  == the C*a0 = const ray
  The gamma(separation) SHAPE gives ZERO breaking here: the whole curve is a
  RIGID one-parameter family in (C*a0).  sigma(ln a0) with C free = INFINITE.
  => In the strictly no-EFE reading (Milgrom arXiv:2503.07106, EFE absent by
     his eq 19-20 -- the reading with the LARGEST WB signal), a0's amplitude is
     STRICTLY unmeasurable from wide binaries.  Only C*a0 is observable.

================================================================================================
D2 -- LAW L2 (EFE-saturated point-field): DOES THE SECOND SCALE BREAK IT?
================================================================================================
  gamma(g) = (1 + C a0/(g + g_extN))^(1/4).  Now there are TWO scales, so the
  curve has an AMPLITUDE (C*a0) and a TRANSITION LOCATION (g_extN).  Test 1:
  treat (C, a0, g_extN) as free -- i.e. forget the framework's own inversion.
  [canonical] eigenvalues [1.490e-14 2.100e+00 1.116e+02]  cond 7.49e+15  singular=True
             null eigenvector (lnC, ln a0, ln gE) = (-0.7071, +0.7071, -0.0000)
  [ALT      ] eigenvalues [4.087e-14 3.124e+00 1.606e+02]  cond 3.93e+15  singular=True
             null eigenvector (lnC, ln a0, ln gE) = (-0.7071, +0.7071, +0.0000)
  READING: still EXACTLY singular along (lnC, ln a0, ln gE) = (+1,-1,0)/sqrt2.
  The second scale breaks the AMPLITUDE-vs-TRANSITION degeneracy -- it does NOT
  break a0-vs-C.  The shape measures g_extN; the amplitude measures C*a0.

  Test 2: impose the framework's OWN inversion g_extN = g_extN(a0, g_ext,obs).
  Now a0 enters BOTH the amplitude and the transition, so the exact null lifts.
  footing     cos(a0,C)   infl.  s(ln a0)|C,gx fix  s(ln a0)|C free  s(ln a0)|gx 2.4%
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  canonical    0.998682    19.5              0.126              2.5             0.128
  ALT          0.998182    16.6              0.103              1.7             0.104
  (columns are FRACTIONAL sigma on a0, i.e. sigma(ln a0); 'gx' = g_ext,obs)
  READING, stated in the framework's favour first: with the prescription C FIXED
  and g_ext PERFECTLY known, the FULL CURVE does formally measure a0, and at
  ~10-13% -- BETTER than the asymptote-only route (D4) because a0 also moves the
  TRANSITION LOCATION, which is genuine extra shape information.  That is the
  strongest honest form of the shape-breaks-it hypothesis and it is granted.
